+ÿU "3›äò!9kÆdç⁄ fÃÚÛ˚ÖÓtΩ∂é5{"url":"/gcsl/implementation/evaluation-state/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/implementation/evaluation-state/","content":"200 Evaluation State. GCSL evaluation uses reference-counted values and explicit execution contexts. A closure captures values; a frame holds the current call's slots. This supports value semantics without exposing shared mutable variables to programs. 200.1 Futures and memo tables # A future begins pending, becomes running during evaluation, and records a successful result. Reentering running work is an error rather than an infinite cycle through the same pending computation. A failed evaluation does not become a cached success. Memo tables belong to the execution context and are keyed by closure identity and argument values, with supported ambient inputs included when used. Keeping tables outside closures avoids introducing reference cycles through cached keys and results. Successful entries for live closures remain until their context ends; they are not an eviction-based cache. 200.2 Exits and cleanup # Runtime errors and language control flow share an unwinding path through the evaluator. A loop handles its own break or continue; a function, future, or labeled block handles a matching return. Intermediate operations restore stack and evaluation state before propagating an exit. A failed REPL input does not commit its new bindings or memo-table changes to the session. Effects already performed outside the interpreter are not rolled back. A file written or a subprocess started before an error still requires the host's ordinary handling. A workspace remembers the first runtime failure of each file. A later request that needs that file receives the original diagnostic without rerunning its effects or starting dependent files. Successful prior files keep their values, and unrelated files can still run. Create a new workspace object when you intentionally need to retry evaluation. 200.3 Resource limits # The parser's maximum expression depth is gcsl::MAX_DEPTH, 10,000 levels. Runtime expressions, calls, and awaits share a nesting budget; calls cost additional levels. Iterations reuse their body's depth rather than adding one level per iteration. Tail calls are not eliminated. These limits protect the recursive implementation, not total computation. A program can still loop indefinitely, allocate large values, capture large process output, or retain many memoized results. The embedding host owns any execution timeout and memory limit. The main implementation lives in eval.rs, value.rs, and session.rs.","word_count":361,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / gcsl-internals","title":"200 Evaluation State"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"futures-and-memo-tables","text":"200.1 Futures and memo tables #","location":34},{"element":"h2","id":"exits-and-cleanup","text":"200.2 Exits and cleanup #","location":130},{"element":"h2","id":"resource-limits","text":"200.3 Resource limits #","location":272}]}},"word_data":{"explicit":[{"position":9,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"bodi":[{"position":301,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":301,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"closur":[{"position":13,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":89,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":104,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":118,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"program":[{"position":33,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":325,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"oper":[{"position":167,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"host":[{"position":216,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":216,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":344,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"recurs":[{"position":319,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"return":[{"position":165,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"captur":[{"position":14,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":333,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"are":[{"position":86,"weight"